<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iconoclasm</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as iconoclasts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iconoclasm is the belief in the importance of destroying icons and other images or monuments, usually for religious or political reasons; it is most often the result of disputes between factions of the same religion. The term comes from the Byzantine Iconoclasm, the struggle over religious icons in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> from 726 to 842 CE, begun when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iii-the-isaurian">Emperor Leo III</a> issued edicts between 726 and 730 against the veneration of images. Earlier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> had ordered references to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> cut from Egyptian tombs and temples. Later episodes include the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and the destruction of monarchist symbols during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word comes from the Greek eikon, &quot;figure&quot;, and klao, &quot;to break&quot;. Those who carry out or support iconoclasm are iconoclasts, and the term is also applied figuratively to anyone who challenges cherished beliefs. Iconoclasm does not generally cover the destruction of the images of a ruler after death or overthrow, which is better known as damnatio memoriae.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> the most significant episode took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-period">Amarna Period</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> sent officials to chisel out references to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> and other deities on tombs, temple walls and cartouches. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, God instructed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> to destroy the engraved stones and molded images of the Canaanites, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> purged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Temple, reforms that his son Manasseh reversed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Iconoclasm may be carried out by adherents of another religion, but it more often results from disputes between factions of the same religion, and it is more common in religions that oppose idolatry, such as the Abrahamic religions. Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was largely aniconic. After the reign of Justinian (527–565) the use of images increased greatly, and a reaction followed. In 695 the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-ii">Justinian II</a> added a full-face image of Christ to imperial gold coins, which led Caliph Abd al-Malik to begin a coinage with lettering only. The Byzantine Iconoclasm, the struggle between supporters and opponents of icons from 726 to 842, gave the term its origin.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the prohibition of figural representation rests on traditions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">Hadith</a> rather than on the Qur'an, and it has not always extended to secular art. The first Muslim act of iconoclasm dates to 630, when statues of Arabian deities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> were destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The first iconoclastic wave of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> in the early 1520s, under Andreas Karlstadt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-muntzer">Thomas Müntzer</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> intervened to calm things. Reformed leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> urged the removal of images by invoking the prohibition of idolatry, and individuals attacked statues in the beeldenstorm across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> in 1566. The Catholic and Lutheran churches defend sacred images.</p><p>From about 1243 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> went through a period of iconoclasm in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-viii">Jayavarman VIII</a> destroyed many Buddhist images. Many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moai">moai</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a> were toppled during civil wars in the 18th century. Political iconoclasm includes the destruction of royal statues and the exhumation of the French kings from Saint-Denis during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iconoclasm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
